      "math_academy_id": "q-237019",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f",
      "knowledge_point": "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector",
      "problem": "Let $p=i+2j+xk$ and $q=3i+6j-3k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
